        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "1cb07f57-68b9-5254-8d87-0091075a21c7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Ringling, Montana; White Sulphur Springs\n- Companies: Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Paul and Pacific Railroad; White Sulphur Springs and Yellowstone Park Railway\n- Systems: transcontinental main line; Pacific Extension\n- Topics: railroad transportation in the United States\n\nFacts:\n- The Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Paul and Pacific Railroad, often called the Milwaukee Road, operated as a Class I railroad in the Midwestern and Northwestern United States from 1847 until 1980.\n- In 1980, the Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Paul and Pacific Railroad abandoned its Pacific Extension in Montana, Idaho, and Washington after a bankruptcy.\n- Ringling, Montana, was a station stop on the transcontinental main line of the Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Paul and Pacific Railroad.\n- Ringling, Montana, was the southern terminus of the White Sulphur Springs and Yellowstone Park Railway.\n- The White Sulphur Springs and Yellowstone Park Railway ran from Ringling to White Sulphur Springs.",
